"Away from the troop and sitting high above the forest floor, these macaques take turns grooming one another. They search through each other's fur, trying to find ticks, lice, and anything crawly that shouldn't be there.": "4a81fdd2da16",
"Away from the troop and sitting high above the forest floor, these macaques take turns grooming one another. They search through each other's fur, trying to find ticks, lice, and anything crawly that shouldn't be there. Grooming has two very important benefits. Firstly, it is quality social time, not unlike the time people spend together talking over coffee. The grooming helps to strengthen bonds between individuals and in turn this helps these very social animals maintain a sense of community. Secondly, grooming is very important for the health of these macaques. A friend can remove nasty ticks and biting insects which they wouldn't normally be able to reach by themselves. Too many parasites on one monkey can lower the immune system and even cause disease and infection. But what does the groomer get out of all of this hard work? What’s in it for him? Well, it seems, this habitual grooming does come with a reward of sorts after all; A big juicy one, in fact. You see, each parasite removed becomes a tasty snack for the grooming macaque to enjoy!": "eb79f2315aea",
